  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "SDR-01",
      "chapter": "lifts",
      "anchor": "lifts-siegel-discrepancy",
      "title": "The averaged one-vector ball count controls the Gaussian in the unbounded cusp",
      "statement": "For one column, level N≥1 and every R>0 the mean count of nonzero vectors is at most C_N(R^k+R^(2k)), where k=[F:ℚ]M. On the reduced region, the absolute Gaussian contribution of all nonzero vectors is O(Y^(−k/2)) for Y=min Im τ_w≥t>0. Multiplication by the modular weight gives a uniform bound when κ Im τ_w≤Y for every w.",
      "hypotheses": "CM E/F, nondegenerate E-Hermitian space of dimension M≥3, normalized quotient Haar probability, fixed F-basis of E. Use all archimedean coordinates and the standard finite coordinate lattices. The Gaussian uses positive definite majorants of the trace form. For each fixed finite locally constant compactly supported test, choose its denominator N. The implied constants may depend on these data and t,κ.",
      "correspondence": "The scalar discrepancy needs one vector in an arbitrary ambient dimension M≥3. The small-radius estimate retains all nonzero isotropic vectors. The packing estimate for large boxes includes zero in its reference box. The dyadic bounds apply throughout the unbounded range of reduced heights.",
