    {
      "id": "SDR-02",
      "chapter": "lifts",
      "anchor": "lifts-siegel-modular-bound",
      "title": "The weighted Eisenstein tail and finite changes of test bound every scalar coefficient",
      "statement": "In the product of Hilbert upper half-planes the weighted absolute difference between the theta average and the Eisenstein series is bounded for each fixed finite test. For the positive Gaussian, standard finite lattice dilated by a⁻¹, and β≠0, removing the weight and extracting the β coefficient gives |I(a)−wP(a)|≤C a^([F:ℚ]M) for every positive integer a.",
      "hypotheses": "The same Hermitian data and coordinate measures as the one-vector estimate, the standard adelic additive character, and M≥3. The finite denominator-ideal series converges because M−1>1. The reduced minimum height is positive. Automorphy transforms both the argument and the finite test.",
      "correspondence": "The constant term φ(0) cancels exactly. The integral part of a reducing matrix moves the test in a uniformly bounded finite span. Integral translates within each denominator class cost ∏y_w^(1−M), leaving ∏y_w^(1−M/2) after weighting. The Fourier integral is divided by the period volume.",
      "dependencies": [
        "SDR-01"
      ],
