    {
      "id": "SDR-03",
      "chapter": "lifts",
      "anchor": "lifts-schwartz-fibre-density",
      "title": "Schwartz decay makes every invertible archimedean Gram fibre integrable",
      "statement": "For a complex Schwartz test Φ on n columns, its integral against the Gram fibre measure m_S exists at every invertible archimedean target. The fibre integrals are continuous there, they disintegrate Φ over each compact regular target set, and scaling source and target Haar by a,a′>0 multiplies them by a/a′.",
      "hypotheses": "Any CM E/F, nondegenerate Hermitian space of dimension M, any n≥0, all archimedean places, additive source and target Haar measures with an associated regular Gram-fibre family. There is no stable-range or M≥3 requirement for this local theorem. The global application separately assumes its stated rank range.",
      "correspondence": "In an open target chart, disintegration yields one reference fibre with finite integral of (1+‖x‖)^(−D−1). A local linear transport with bounded inverse provides the same dominator for every nearby test. Compact cutoffs then recover disintegration for the complex test. The global caller applies the lemma at both the original and rescaled measures.",
      "dependencies": [
        "GFG-02",
        "GFG-03"
      ],
      "equations": [],
